<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/intermediate/biodiversity-and-conservation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Conservation biology is <strong>ecology with a deadline</strong>: documenting what is being
    lost, at what rate, from which causes — and testing which interventions actually reverse the
    trends. It is the discipline where the biology ladder\'s knowledge becomes triage.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the census, the extinction rate, the drivers, the interventions" widget="equationStep" config={bioCfg} />

  <h2>Findings that guide the field</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Species–area relationships power the estimates:</strong> habitat loss predicts species loss via a curve (island biogeography, MacArthur & Wilson) — the "extinction debt": species committed to extinction by past habitat loss haven\'t all died yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Successes exist and teach:</strong> the ozone layer is recovering (Montreal Protocol — the environmental-economics lesson); whales recovered after the moratorium; the California condor and Mauritius kestrel came back from the edge. Directed action works.</li>
    <li><strong>Rewilding and trophic cascades:</strong> Yellowstone\'s wolves (the ecology lesson) proved top predators reorganise ecosystems — rewilding is restoration with the food web, not just the plants.</li>
    <li><strong>The funding asymmetry:</strong> global conservation spending (~$20B/yr) versus the biodiversity funding gap (~$700B/yr) — why economists design debt-for-nature swaps and conservation finance (the advanced lessons).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IPBES Global Assessment (2019); Pimm et al., Science; MacArthur & Wilson, The Theory of Island Biogeography (1967); Kunming-Montreal GBF (2022).</p>
</TopicPage>
